#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acc86fb6-2ef4-5f8f-b204-6e1fdbbf0530" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cafdb077-0e02-5e7a-bf78-c3a8aacfb6f7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Angle or Side of a Triangle Given the Area of a Triangle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd3dcff-06d2-5d61-9863-0f5b541d8d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107027 :tutorial/content "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC},$ as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah.\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e126d83f-0c01-514b-bb7e-ce2d16291b22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13277 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Area of a General Triangle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107063 :tutorial/content "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides $BC = \\color{red}{a}$ and $AC ={\\color{blue}{b}}.$ For the triangle above we have\n${\\color{red}a=10},$ ${\\color{blue}b=12},$ and  $m\\angle C=45^{\\circ}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ff9f9791-f688-59c5-8c9d-b776016045be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2465 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
